#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""dt-api-set-crew-password — give the crew login a new password that only `dtapi` holds.

B-CREDENTIALS-PRIVATE-REPO OBJ-1 (#1023). Pre-audit §2.2 item 4 (r4), with Langston's
Step-2 conditions C2, C3, C4 and C7. Run BY HAND, as root, on staging:

    PGPASSFILE=/root/<run-only file> /usr/local/sbin/dt-api-set-crew-password

PGPASSFILE is a root-owned 0600 file holding ONE libpq line, host:port:db:user:password,
made for this run by the operator (the change list says how). The setter reads it and
REMOVES it on exit. ⛔ Neither this nor dt-api loads anything from /home/deploy.

WHAT IT DOES, in order — the numbers are the pre-audit's:
  budget  refuses unless the shared login ledger records NO loopback login in the last 900 s
  (0)     if a marker from an interrupted run exists: reconcile it, report, and STOP
  (1)     save a marker (old hash, old role) and a copy of the live env, both root 0600
  (1b)    write the new value to a temp env beside the real one, 0600 dtapi:dtapi
  (2)     COMMIT: password AND role='editor' for the crew row, asserting exactly 1 row and
          RETURNING role = 'editor' (a password-only write FAILS here — the mutation test)
  (3)     a fresh login with the new value: token issued after the start, user.role 'editor'
  (4)     THE COMMIT POINT: rename the temp env into place
  (5)     purge dt-api's token and negative cache, then release dt-api's lock
  (6)     `sudo -n -u dtapi dt-api GET /api/settings` must succeed — the path callers use
  (7)     delete the marker and the old-env copy, LAST

dt-api's lock is held from before (0) through (5). A failure before (4) restores the hash and
role from the marker; a failure at (5) or (6) restores the hash, role AND env together.
STATED RESIDUAL: between (2) and a restore the database holds the new hash while the live env
holds the old value — a non-atomic window, bounded by this run.

EXIT: 0 done (or reconciled) · 1 failed and restored · 2 refused, nothing touched (an earlier
        run's psql still connected included) ·
      3 stopped before anything it could not undo (an answer it could not use, an interrupt at a
        checkpoint — restored first where needed —, an unexpected error) — any marker is
        reconciled by the next run ·
      4 a person must look: a restore failed, the recovery data (the marker) is unreadable, the
        crew row was changed by someone else mid-run, or an env names another user — the marker
        is KEPT; do not delete it
SIGNALS: SIGHUP is ignored (a dropped ssh does not stop a run). SIGTERM/SIGINT are acted on only
at the checkpoints before (4); after the commit point the run finishes and reports.
"""

